--
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-84-## Detailed Building and Installation
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-85-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:86:STP is built with [CMake](https://cmake.org/), version 3.0.2 or newer. CMake is a
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-87-meta build system that generates build files for other tools such as
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-88-make(1), Visual Studio, Xcode, etc.
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-89-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-90-### Configuration variables
--
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-92-generators.
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-93-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:94:- `CMAKE_BUILD_TYPE` - The build type (e.g. Release)
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:95:- `CMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX` - The prefix for install (e.g. /usr/local )
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-96-- `ENABLE_ASSERTIONS` - If TRUE STP will be built with asserts.
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-97-- `ENABLE_TESTING` - Enable running tests
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-98-- `ENABLE_PYTHON_INTERFACE` - Enable building the Python interface
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-99-- `PYTHON_EXECUTABLE` - Set python executable in case you have more than one python installed
--
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-101-- `STATICCOMPILE` - Build static libraries and binaries instead of dynamic
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-102-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:103:### Dependencies
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:104:STP relies on : boost, flex, bison and minisat. You can install these by:
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-105-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-106-```
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:107:$ sudo apt-get install cmake bison flex libboost-all-dev python perl minisat
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-108-```
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-109-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:110:If your distribution does not come with minisat, STP maintains an updated fork. It can be built as follows:
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-111-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-112-```
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:113:$ git clone https://github.com/stp/minisat
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:114:$ cd minisat
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-115-$ mkdir build && cd build
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:116:$ cmake ..
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:117:$ cmake --build .
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:118:$ sudo cmake --install .
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-119-$ command -v ldconfig && sudo ldconfig
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-120-```
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-121-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:122:STP uses minisat as its SAT solver by default but it also supports other SAT solvers including CryptoMiniSat as an optional 
extra. If installed, it will be detected during the cmake and used:
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-123-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-124-```
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:125:$ git clone https://github.com/msoos/cryptominisat
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:126:$ cd cryptominisat
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-127-$ mkdir build && cd build
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:128:$ cmake ..
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:129:$ cmake --build .
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:130:$ sudo cmake --install .
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-131-$ command -v ldconfig && sudo ldconfig
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-132-```
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-133-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:134:Alternatively, these commands are pre-configused in `scripts/deps/setup-minisat.sh` and `scripts/deps/setup-cms.sh` 
(respectively).
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-135-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-136-#### Building against non-installed libraries
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-137-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:138:If you wish to build STP's dependencies without installing them, you can tell CMake where to find the non-installed artefacts. 
For example:
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-139-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:140:* `-DMINISAT_INCLUDE_DIRS:PATH=<path>` and `-DMINISAT_LIBDIR:PATH=<path>` -- the paths to `minisat/core/Solver.h` and the 
`minisat` libraries (respectively)
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:141:* `-Dcryptominisat5_DIR:PATH=<path>` -- the path to `cryptominisat5Config.cmake`
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-142-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:143:If you did not install these development libraries, then `MINISAT_LIBDIR` can be set to your `build` folder for minisat and 
`cryptominisat5_DIR` to your `build` folder for CryptoMiniSat.
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-144-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-145-### Building a static library and binary
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-146-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-147-```
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-148-$ mkdir build && cd build
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:149:$ cmake -DSTATICCOMPILE=ON ..
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:150:$ cmake --build .
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:151:$ sudo cmake --install .
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-152-$ command -v ldconfig && sudo ldconfig
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-153-```
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-154-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-155-### Configuration and build options
--
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-157-To tweak the build configuration:
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-158-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:159:* Run `cmake-gui /path/to/stp/source/root` instead of `cmake`. This
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-160-  user interface lets you control the value of various configuration
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-161-  variables and lets you pick the build system generator.
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-162-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:163:* Run `ccmake` instead of `cmake`. This provides an ncurses terminal
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-164-  interface for changing configuration variables.
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-165-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown:166:* Pass `-D<VARIABLE>=<VALUE>` options to `cmake` (not very user friendly).
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-167-  It is probably best if you **only** configure this way if you are writing
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-168-  scripts.
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-169-
/tmp/stp-src/README.markdown-170-You can also tweak configuration later by running `make edit_cache` and edit any configuration variables, reconfigure and then 
regenerate the build system. After configuration, build by running `make`.
